<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrrhus of Epirus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">319–272 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyrrhus was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, sheltered as an infant by the Taulantian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaukias">Glaukias</a>. Invited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> in 280 BCE, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at Heraclea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> at such cost that a 'Pyrrhic victory' became proverbial. Checked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> in 275 BCE, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> in 272 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Pyrrhus of Epirus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
